1. Use device-scope release/acquire atomics to publish the payload between workgroups. Relaxed operations alone do not establish a happens-before relationship for the non-atomic payload.

```cpp
#include <hip/hip_runtime.h>

struct Shared {
    int payload;
    int flag;
};

__global__ void producer(Shared* s) {
    s->payload = 7;
    __hip_atomic_store(&s->flag, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE,
                       __HIP_MEMORY_SCOPE_AGENT);
}

__global__ void consumer(Shared* s, int* result) {
    while (__hip_atomic_load(&s->flag, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE,
                             __HIP_MEMORY_SCOPE_AGENT) == 0) {
    }
    *result = s->payload;
}

void launch(Shared* s, int* result,
            hipStream_t producerStream, hipStream_t consumerStream) {
    hipLaunchKernelGGL(consumer, dim3(1), dim3(1), 0,
                       consumerStream, s, result);
    hipLaunchKernelGGL(producer, dim3(1), dim3(1), 0,
                       producerStream, s);
}
```

Compile with hipcc. Allocate `s` and `result` in device memory, initialize `flag` to zero and finish initialization before calling `launch`; use distinct nonblocking streams, check HIP errors, and synchronize both streams before retrieving the result or freeing memory. Under the stated concurrent-residency assumption, the consumer can make progress. Agent scope covers workgroups on the same GPU; participation by a CPU or another GPU would require an appropriate wider scope and supported memory configuration.

When the acquire load observes the release store's value, the preceding payload write happens-before the consumer's payload read. A fence alone does not repair this: a producer-only fence supplies no acquiring synchronization on the consumer. A correctly paired release fence before the relaxed store and acquire fence after the successful relaxed load can establish synchronization under the atomic fence rules, but release/acquire operations express the protocol more directly.

2. No—persisting deduplication records on both replicas does not establish exclusive execution authority.

Take a non-idempotent increment with request ID X. During a partition, replica A sees no local record for X, persists its record, and executes the increment; replica B independently does the same. After healing, both confirm that X is durably recorded, so the acknowledgment condition passes, but the update has executed twice. For a shared counter, it has increased by two; for separate replica-local state, both replicas have executed X, and the eventual logical result depends on the reconciliation rules. The acknowledgment rule prevents neither execution.

The necessary invariants are:
• Each request has a stable identity, with its operation contents bound to that identity.
• There is one authoritative decision and execution path for that identity. Typically this is a quorum-backed leader or equivalent serialized authority; partitioned replicas cannot both independently execute.
• Authority changes fence stale executors at the resource applying the effect. A leader designation alone is insufficient if an old leader can still perform writes.
• Applying the effect and recording its committed outcome are crash-atomic, or recoverably coupled by a durable transaction/log. Recovery must distinguish committed effects from intents and must never blindly replay an ambiguous non-idempotent effect.
• Recovery preserves committed deduplication state, retries reuse X, and reclamation excludes all possible stale retries.

If the effect is external and cannot participate in that transaction, the effect recipient must itself enforce request-ID deduplication or an equivalent atomic protocol. Merely replicating an intent cannot resolve a crash between executing the effect and recording its completion.

3. Expand edges in parallel, not one whole adjacency list per vertex. Otherwise the vertex owning half the edges creates a linear critical path.

Assume a duplicate-free frontier F, immutable CSR adjacency, and a persistent atomic `visited` array initialized before BFS. Let f = |F| and E_F be the number of outgoing edges from this frontier.

First, compute frontier degrees and exclusive-scan them to obtain adjacency offsets. Allocate E_F edge slots. For each nonempty frontier adjacency list, mark its starting slot with that frontier vertex's index; initialize other slots to −1. A parallel prefix-maximum scan identifies the owner of every slot. Slot j can then calculate its CSR edge address in constant time from its owner and that owner's offset. Empty adjacency lists emit no markers, so coincident offsets cause no collisions.

Process all E_F slots in parallel. For destination v, perform an atomic compare-and-swap of `visited[v]` from zero to one. Exactly one successful claimant writes v into its private candidate slot; all other slots are marked invalid. Scan the validity bits and compact the successful candidates into the next frontier. Initialize the source's visited bit before the first expansion. If distances or parents are needed, only the winner writes them, and finish the entire expansion before consuming the next frontier.

For an explicit contention model, assume unrelated atomic addresses progress independently, but k operations to one address serialize in Θ(k) time, with constant work per operation and no retry loops. Let C be the maximum number of this expansion's edges targeting any one vertex.

The degree scan, slot initialization, owner scan, edge processing, and compaction each use O(f + E_F) total work. Their span is O(log(2 + f + E_F) + C): scans contribute logarithmic depth, and the most-contended atomic address contributes C. Zero-edge frontiers terminate without edge processing. These bounds assume enough processors; on P processors, an ideal scheduler gives O((f + E_F)/P + log(2 + f + E_F) + C) time under this contention model.

Across the BFS, each reached vertex enters a frontier once and each outgoing edge of a reached vertex is inspected once. Including initial visited-array setup, total work is O(n + m). The heavy owner's edges are distributed across threads, but duplicate suppression can still have linear span if all those edges target the same vertex; the contention term explicitly captures that limitation.
